.TH SSL_CLIENT 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
ssl_client \- pipe data through TLS on a connected socket
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ssl_client
.RB [ \-ce ]
.BI "\-h " handle
.RB [ \-n
.IR name ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ssl_client
is the TLS helper of
.BR wget (1).
It performs a TLS client handshake on a TCP connection that its parent
opened, then copies data in both directions.
Standard input is encrypted and sent to the server.
What the server sends is decrypted and written to standard output.
Both are binary.
.PP
It cannot connect by itself: the parent gives it the socket as a Windows
handle with
.BR \-h .
To fetch an https or ftps URL,
.B wget
opens the socket, then runs
.B ssl_client
with
.B \-h
and
.B \-n
(the host name from the URL) and a pipe as standard input and output,
and talks to the pipe.
The kit's shell and tools have no way to give it a socket, so it is not
run by hand.
.PP
Windows does the TLS, through Schannel, and checks certificates against
the Windows certificate store; see
.BR \-c .
.SS Copying
.B ssl_client
waits until standard input or the connection has data.
It reads standard input in pieces of up to 64 KiB, and encrypts and
sends each piece at once, in TLS records of up to 16 KiB.
A piece longer than 16 KiB is corrupted: each record after the first
repeats the start of the piece instead of continuing it, so the server
receives the right number of bytes but the wrong ones.
Input from a file larger than 16 KiB is always affected, and input from
a pipe whenever more than 16 KiB is waiting in it.
.PP
It reads the connection differently.
It keeps reading and decrypting until it holds 64 KiB of plain text, the
server ends the TLS session, or the connection closes, and only then
writes to standard output.
A short reply therefore appears only when the server ends the session.
That suits HTTP with
.BR "Connection: close" ,
as
.B wget
sends it, but not a protocol in which the client waits for each reply
before it sends more.
FTP is such a protocol, so
.B wget
fails on an ftps URL: the server's greeting reaches it only when the
server gives up and closes the connection.
While it waits for the connection,
.B ssl_client
does not look at standard input.
.SS Ending
.B ssl_client
exits with status 0 when the session ends, which happens in one of these
ways:
.IP \(bu 2
The server closes the connection or ends the TLS session.
The data received so far is written first.
A connection closed without ending the TLS session is not reported, so a
truncated reply goes unnoticed.
.IP
A read of the connection collects data up to the server's close alert,
so the alert usually comes with data.
Then
.B ssl_client
writes the data, but answers the alert, and exits, only when the
connection is next readable or standard input ends.
A server that waits for the answer before it closes the connection
therefore delays the exit until standard input ends.
Input that arrives in the meantime is an error:
.BR "schannel: attempted to write to a closed connection" .
.IP \(bu 2
Standard input reaches end of file, or reading it fails.
.B ssl_client
sends a TLS
.B close_notify
alert.
With
.BR \-e ,
it then exits at once.
Without
.BR \-e ,
it waits until the connection is readable, such as when the server
closes it, then exits.
Whatever arrives from the server after the alert is discarded, so the
reply to a request sent just before end of file is lost.
Keep standard input open until the reply has arrived.
.SH OPTIONS
Options may be grouped, as in
.BR \-ce ,
and the argument of
.B \-h
or
.B \-n
may be attached or be the next argument.
.B \-\-
ends the options, and operands are ignored.
.TP
.B \-c
Do not validate the server's certificate.
Without
.BR \-c ,
Schannel checks the certificate chain against the Windows certificate
store, and the certificate's names against the one given with
.BR \-n ,
and the handshake fails if either check fails.
With
.BR \-c ,
any certificate is accepted.
.IP
.B wget
passes
.B \-c
for an https URL when it is given
.BR \-\-no\-check\-certificate ,
and never for an ftps URL.
.TP
.B \-e
Exit as soon as standard input reaches end of file, after sending the
TLS
.B close_notify
alert, instead of waiting for the connection to become readable.
.B wget
passes
.B \-e
for the control connection of an ftps URL.
.TP
.BI "\-h " handle
The connected socket to use, as the value of its Windows handle in
hexadecimal digits.
Upper and lower case and leading zeros are accepted.
The value ends at the first character that is not a hexadecimal digit,
so a
.B 0x
prefix reads as handle 0.
This option is required: without it, or with a value that begins with no
hexadecimal digit, the message is
.B ssl_client: invalid handle
and the exit status is 1.
.IP
The handle is not checked before use.
It must be valid in this process, so the socket must be inheritable.
It must have been created without overlapped I/O, as busybox creates
sockets.
A handle that is not open fails with
.BR "write error: Bad file descriptor" ,
and an overlapped socket with
.BR "write error: Invalid argument" .
.TP
.BI "\-n " name
The server name.
Schannel checks the certificate against it, and sends it as the server
name indication (SNI) unless it is an IP address.
It may be an IP address if the certificate names that address.
Without
.BR \-n ,
no name is sent, and unless
.B \-c
is given the handshake fails because the certificate matches no name.
.B wget
passes the host name from the URL, without a port.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage message on standard output and exit.
It is recognized only as the first argument.
.PP
An unknown option or a missing option argument prints a message and the
usage on standard error and exits with status 1.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.BR LC_ALL ", " LC_CTYPE ", " LANG
If the first of these that is set has the value
.BR C ,
output to a console is not converted, as described in
.BR NOTES ,
and the console interprets the bytes in its own code page.
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
Read by the C library's option parser.
When set, option parsing stops at the first operand, so options after it
are ignored.
.PP
Variables that affect every busybox applet, such as
.B BB_TERMINAL_MODE
for console output, are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The session ended as described under
.BR Ending ,
or the usage was requested with
.BR \-\-help .
.TP
.B 1
An invalid command line or handle, a handshake that failed, including one
that failed certificate validation, a TLS or connection error, or an
error writing to the connection or to standard output.
The message is on standard error, prefixed with
.BR ssl_client: .
.TP
.B 141
Standard output is a pipe that was closed before all output was written.
.SH NOTES
Differences from busybox on Unix:
.IP \(bu 2
The options are different.
Unix
.B ssl_client
has
.BR \-s ,
.BR \-r ,
a host operand, and
.B \-e
to run a program, and none of them exist here.
Here
.B \-e
is a flag, and
.B \-c
and
.B \-h
are new.
.IP \(bu 2
The TLS code of Unix busybox, which cannot validate certificates, is not
built.
Schannel is used instead, and it validates.
.PP
Schannel is asked for TLS 1.0, 1.1, and 1.2 with strong cryptography.
TLS 1.3 is not enabled in this build, so a connection to a server that
supports both TLS 1.2 and TLS 1.3 uses TLS 1.2.
The Windows version and its policy decide which protocols and cipher
suites are in fact available.
No client certificate is sent, and revocation checking is not requested.
.PP
A handshake that fails is reported as
.BI "schannel: handshake failed: (" status "): " text
with the Schannel status in hexadecimal and Windows's text for it.
Two common statuses are
.B 0x80090325
(the certificate chain was issued by an authority that is not trusted)
and
.B 0x80090322
(the target principal name is incorrect).
A server that closes the connection during the handshake gives
.BR "schannel: handshake read() failed" .
.PP
Standard input and output are binary, so bytes such as CR LF, Ctrl-Z,
and NUL pass unchanged.
When standard output is a console, busybox converts text for display and
lets escape sequences take effect, as for other applets.
.PP
There is no timeout other than the ones Windows applies.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR nc (1),
.BR wget (1)
